Under the [[bijection|1-to-1 correspondence]] between matrices and linear maps, matrix multiplication corresponds to [[function composition|composition]] of maps:<ref>{{harvtxt|Greub|1975|p=90}}. Note however that Greub follows a transposed convention of representing a transformation by multiplying a row vector by a matrix, rather than multiplying a matrix by a column vector, leading to the reversed order for the two matrices in the product that represents a composition.</ref> if a {{mvar|k}}-by-{{mvar|m}} matrix {{math|'''B'''}} represents another linear map {{tmath|1= g: \R^m \to \R^k }}, then the composition {{math|''g'' ∘ ''f''}} is represented by {{math|'''BA'''}} since{{sfnp|Lang|1986|loc=§VI.1}}
<math display="block">(g \circ f)(\bold x) = g(f(\bold x)) = g(\bold{Ax}) = \bold B(\bold {Ax}) = (\bold{BA}) \bold x.</math>

The last equality follows from the above-mentioned associativity of matrix multiplication.

The [[rank of a matrix]] {{math|'''A'''}} is the maximum number of [[linear independence|linearly independent]] row vectors of the matrix, which is the same as the maximum number of linearly independent column vectors.{{sfnp|Brown|1991|loc=Definition II.3.3}} Equivalently it is the [[Hamel dimension|dimension]] of the [[image (mathematics)|image]] of the linear map represented by {{math|'''A'''}}.{{sfnp|Greub|1975|loc=Section III.1}} The [[rank–nullity theorem]] states that the dimension of the [[kernel (matrix)|kernel]] of a matrix plus the rank equals the number of columns of the matrix.{{sfnp|Brown|1991|loc=Theorem II.3.22}}

== Square matrix ==
{{Main|Square matrix}}
A [[square matrix]] is a matrix with the same number of rows and columns. An {{mvar|n}}-by-{{mvar|n}} matrix is known as a square matrix of order {{mvar|n}}. Any two square matrices of the same order can be added and multiplied.
The entries {{mvar|a{{sub|ii}}}} form the [[main diagonal]] of a square matrix. They lie on the imaginary line running from the top left corner to the bottom right corner of the matrix.{{sfnp|Anton|2010|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=YmcQJoFyZ5gC&pg=PA27 27]}}

Square matrices of a given dimension form a [[noncommutative ring]], which is one of the most common examples of a noncommutative ring.{{sfnp|Reyes|2025}}

