==== Diagonal and triangular matrix ====
If all entries of {{math|'''A'''}} below the main diagonal are zero, {{math|'''A'''}} is called an ''upper [[triangular matrix]]''. Similarly, if all entries of {{math|'''A'''}} above the main diagonal are zero, {{math|'''A'''}} is called a ''lower triangular matrix''.{{sfnp|Anton|2010|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=YmcQJoFyZ5gC&pg=PA68 68]}} If all entries outside the main diagonal are zero, {{math|'''A'''}} is called a [[diagonal matrix]].{{sfnp|Gbur|2011|p=91}}

==== Identity matrix ====
{{Main|Identity matrix}}
The ''identity matrix'' {{math|'''I'''{{sub|''n''}}}} of size {{mvar|n}} is the {{mvar|n}}-by-{{mvar|n}} matrix in which all the elements on the [[main diagonal]] are equal to {{math|1}} and all other elements are equal to {{math|0}},{{sfnp|Boas|2005|p=118}} for example,
<math display=block>\begin{align}
  \mathbf{I}_1 &= \begin{bmatrix} 1 \end{bmatrix}, \\[4pt]
  \mathbf{I}_2 &= \begin{bmatrix}
1 & 0 \\
0 & 1
\end{bmatrix}, \\[4pt]
  \vdots& \\[4pt]
  \mathbf{I}_n &= \begin{bmatrix}
     1 &      0 & \cdots & 0 \\
     0 &      1 & \cdots & 0 \\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
     0 &      0 & \cdots & 1
\end{bmatrix}
\end{align}</math>
It is a square matrix of order {{mvar|n}}, and also a special kind of [[diagonal matrix]]. It is called an identity matrix because multiplication with it leaves a matrix unchanged:{{sfnp|Boas|2005|p=118}}
<math display=block>
\bold{AI}_n = \bold I_m \bold A = \bold A</math>
for any {{mvar|m}}-by-{{mvar|n}} matrix {{math|'''A'''}}.

A scalar multiple of an identity matrix is called a ''scalar'' matrix.{{sfnp|Horn|Johnson|1985|loc=§0.9.1 Diagonal matrices}} 

==== Symmetric or skew-symmetric matrix ====
A square matrix {{math|'''A'''}} that is equal to its transpose, that is, {{math|1='''A''' = '''A'''{{sup|T}}}}, is a [[symmetric matrix]]. If instead, {{math|'''A'''}} is equal to the negative of its transpose, that is, {{math|1='''A''' = −'''A'''{{sup|T}}}}, then {{math|'''A'''}} is a [[skew-symmetric matrix]]. In complex matrices, symmetry is often replaced by the concept of [[Hermitian matrix|Hermitian matrices]], which satisfies {{math|1='''A'''{{sup|∗}} = '''A'''}}, where the star or [[asterisk]] denotes the [[conjugate transpose]] of the matrix, that is, the transpose of the [[complex conjugate]] of {{math|'''A'''}}.{{sfnp|Boas|2005|p=138}}

By the [[spectral theorem]], real symmetric matrices and complex Hermitian matrices have an [[eigenbasis]]; that is, every vector is expressible as a [[linear combination]] of eigenvectors. In both cases, all eigenvalues are real.{{sfnp|Horn|Johnson|1985|loc=Theorem 2.5.6}} This theorem can be generalized to infinite-dimensional situations related to matrices with infinitely many rows and columns.{{sfnp|Conway|1990|pp=262–263}}

==== Invertible matrix and its inverse ====
A square matrix {{math|'''A'''}} is called ''[[invertible matrix|invertible]]'' or ''non-singular'' if there exists a matrix {{math|'''B'''}} such that{{sfnp|Brown|1991|loc=Definition I.2.28}}{{sfnp|Brown|1991|loc=Definition I.5.13}}
<math display=block>\bold{AB} = \bold{BA} = \bold I_n,</math>
where {{math|'''I'''{{sub|''n''}}}} is the {{times|{{mvar|n}}|{{mvar|n}}}} [[identity matrix]] with {{math|1}} for each entry on the [[main diagonal]] and {{math|0}} elsewhere. If {{math|'''B'''}} exists, it is unique and is called the ''[[Invertible matrix|inverse matrix]]'' of {{math|'''A'''}}, denoted {{math|'''A'''{{sup|−1}}}}.{{sfnp|Anton|2010|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=YmcQJoFyZ5gC&pg=PA62 62]}}

There are many [[algorithm]]s for testing whether a square matrix is invertible, and, if it is, computing its inverse. One of the oldest, which is still in common use is [[Gaussian elimination]].{{sfnp|Gbur|2011|pp=99–100}}

